\section{Typical workflow}{

A package function that starts asynchronous work may return a
\code{fabric_operation} handle. Use \code{fabric_operation_wait()} to wait for it to
finish and \code{fabric_operation_result()} to retrieve its output. Result
retrieval waits by default, so it is enough for the common case

If the R process restarts, save the handle's \code{id} or the service-provided
\code{location} and pass that value with fresh authentication arguments
}

\section{Results and failures}{

\code{fabric_operation_status()} preserves Fabric's status, progress, timestamps,
request identifiers, and structured error. Status values added by Fabric in
the future remain inspectable, but \code{fabric_operation_wait()} stops with a
typed error instead of polling an unfamiliar value indefinitely

A failed operation raises \code{fabric_operation_failed} by default. A timeout
raises \code{fabric_operation_timeout}; neither condition repeats the request that
originally started the operation
}

\section{Regional operation endpoints}{

Fabric can return a \code{Location} on a regional \verb{*.analysis.windows.net}
cluster. fabricQueryR recognizes those Microsoft endpoints and automatically
uses the Power BI token audience they require. Normal automatic sign-in or an
audience-aware token-provider function handles both audiences. A single
static Fabric bearer token cannot authenticate a regional operation URL
}

